WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.480
You write a component, you call a hook, you trigger a render.

2
00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:08.480
Whether you're building for a web browser or a react native mobile app,

3
00:00:08.480 --> 00:00:12.240
the core syntax of React remains completely identical,

4
00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:15.360
but the environment hosting that code changes entirely.

5
00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:19.520
Web browsers provide an invisible layer of safety nets.

6
00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.760
They handle memory, cache data, and manage network connections automatically.

7
00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:27.520
A mobile operating system strips all of that away.

8
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:30.720
On mobile, there is no window object to rely on.

9
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.800
You lose local storage, you lose automatic tab focus events,

10
00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:38.240
and you can no longer assume your user has a stable internet connection.

11
00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:43.520
Success on mobile requires manually replacing the browser's implicit infrastructure

12
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.280
with explicit mobile equivalents.

13
00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:49.360
This chart shows the three layers of state we must manage,

14
00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.120
local, global, and network.

15
00:00:51.760 --> 00:00:57.120
To make our React code thrive on a phone, we have to fill in these blank spaces on the mobile side,

16
00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.280
mapping web architectures to mobile first principles, layer by layer.

17
00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:05.680
These highly optimized web state architectures remain our foundation.

18
00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:08.160
We are docking them into a new host.

19
00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:09.840
By the time we fill this map,

20
00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.840
we will have constructed a robust offline first mobile state system.

21
00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:18.880
Skip this step, and even the most elegantly written React components will degrade

22
00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.400
into a brittle, disynced, and laggy mess on physical devices.

23
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.280
Looking at the first row of our matrix,

24
00:01:25.280 --> 00:01:28.960
local state tools survive the transition without any modification.

25
00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.520
Both you state and use reducer run directly within React's memory space.

26
00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.160
They require zero adaptation from the web developer.

27
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.960
The context API also ports over,

28
00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:41.120
functioning identically across both platforms.

29
00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:45.360
However, mobile rendering engines are far less forgiving of unnecessary

30
00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:47.840
re-renders than web engines like V8.

31
00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.840
If a state update at the top of a context tree triggers nested re-renders,

32
00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:53.760
a web browser might muscle through it.

33
00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.480
A mobile device will stutter and drop frames.

34
00:01:56.480 --> 00:01:59.760
Therefore, context on mobile requires strict discipline.

35
00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:03.920
It must be isolated to low velocity read heavy state.

36
00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.040
In a React native app, reserve context exclusively for structural constants,

37
00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.200
toggling a dark mode theme, setting an i18 and language preference,

38
00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:15.520
or holding an authentication session.

39
00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:18.880
The tools in this first layer remain exactly the same,

40
00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.400
but the hardware running them dictates a much thinner margin for error.

41
00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:24.560
Moving to global state,

42
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:28.400
porting a standard zoo-stand implementation to mobile immediately fails.

43
00:02:28.880 --> 00:02:34.160
Global state hydration crashes because local storage simply does not exist on a mobile OS.

44
00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:37.440
We need a new database connection to persist our state.

45
00:02:38.000 --> 00:02:42.640
For years, developers used a tool called Async Storage as the historical workaround.

46
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:48.320
Comparing storage solutions, Async Storage relies on a slow asynchronous bridge.

47
00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.640
When an app launches and hydrates its state,

48
00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.600
it waits on a promise, blocking the UI with layout shifts.

49
00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:58.160
Modern mobile architectures replace this entirely with MMKV.

50
00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:04.640
MMKV stores data using memory mapped files and communicates through a C++ JavaScript interface.

51
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:08.080
This completely bypasses the traditional asynchronous bridge.

52
00:03:08.080 --> 00:03:10.640
The measurable outcome is extreme speed.

53
00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:14.240
MMKV is up to 30 times faster than asynchronous.

54
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.360
That execution speed allows it to operate synchronously,

55
00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.640
making it a true one-to-one replacement for web storage.

56
00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:23.680
To implement this, you keep your existing zoo-stand code,

57
00:03:23.680 --> 00:03:28.320
and simply swap out local storage for MMKV as the back end for your persistence middleware.

58
00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:32.400
Bypassing the asynchronous bridge is mandatory for achieving web-like,

59
00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:36.160
instant state hydration, the moment a user launches your application.

60
00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.160
The final matrix row is network state, managed by TANstack Query.

61
00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:42.480
Lacking automatic HTTP caching,

62
00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.760
mobile requires developer to find API revalidation,

63
00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:48.960
unlike browsers that navigate across flat horizontal tabs,

64
00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:52.320
mobile apps push new screens onto a vertical Z-axis stack,

65
00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:54.160
obscuring underlying screens.

66
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.000
TANstacks default refetch on window-focused strategy

67
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.600
fails completely on a phone because tabs do not exist.

68
00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.680
We replace it using react navigations use focus effect hook.

69
00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.320
You explicitly map TANstacks focus manager

70
00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:11.120
to these native mobile screen lifecycle events.

71
00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:14.720
Whenever a user navigates backward and pops a screen off the stack,

72
00:04:14.720 --> 00:04:18.080
the newly revealed screen instantly revalidates any stale data.

73
00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:22.240
Mobile API revalidation must be bound to spatial screen depth,

74
00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.320
rather than flat window visibility.

75
00:04:24.320 --> 00:04:28.880
We also have to address the web's foundational assumption of an always-on connection.

76
00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:31.760
A browser's standard navigator.online API

77
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.720
cannot accurately detect when a device enters a subway tunnel

78
00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.560
or drops a cellular signal.

79
00:04:36.560 --> 00:04:41.680
We adapt by integrating the native at-react native community slash net info library.

80
00:04:41.680 --> 00:04:44.240
By injecting net info into TANstack Query,

81
00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:46.800
the state engine actively monitors connectivity,

82
00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.600
automatically pausing and resuming data mutations based on true physical hardware states.

83
00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.000
By combining TANstack, MMKV, and net info,

84
00:04:56.000 --> 00:04:59.360
we build a secure offline first cache.

85
00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:04.080
The app securely serves and queues data even when the device is in airplane mode.

86
00:05:04.720 --> 00:05:10.880
Mobile applications must treat offline status as a standard expected operating condition,

87
00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:12.800
rather than an error state.

88
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:17.680
With all three layers translated, our architectural map is fully populated.

89
00:05:17.680 --> 00:05:20.080
This diagram shows the final flow.

90
00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:25.280
TANstack Query and ZooStant route their data into a central MMKV core,

91
00:05:25.280 --> 00:05:28.240
orchestrated by a native net info listener.

92
00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:32.960
We completed this transition simply by substituting the environment's infrastructure,

93
00:05:32.960 --> 00:05:35.200
not by changing React itself.

94
00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:38.160
We didn't invent new state management paradigms.

95
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.280
We just built strict environmental adapters.

96
00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.960
Mastery of cross-platform development is defined by our control over environmental constraints,

97
00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:50.560
far beyond the initial memorization of components and tax.

98
00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:56.240
We replaced asynchronous legacy storage with synchronous C++ backed execution.

99
00:05:56.240 --> 00:06:01.600
We manually bound API cache invalidation to physical screen life cycles

100
00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:02.960
and real network states.

101
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:07.280
The result is a system that uses familiar React patterns

102
00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:11.520
to deliver native mobile performance and absolute offline reliability.

103
00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:15.280
If you already know how to build architectures on the web,

104
00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.880
you possess the knowledge required to build elite mobile applications.

105
00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.840
You just need to take explicit control of the new environment.

